    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M6 V07: The motor effect. Why does a current-carrying wire feel a magnetic force? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "Let's look from above. From up here, the field points away from us, into the screen, so we draw it as crosses, just as in earlier videos. Now zoom into the rod. The metal ions stay put, but free electrons drift slowly along it, the opposite way to the conventional current. Each drifting electron is a moving charge in a magnetic field, so each one feels a sideways push. The electrons can't leave the metal, so they push on the rest of the rod, and the whole rod moves. A neutral wire feels a force because of the moving charges inside it. So how big is the total force? Say a charge Q flows through the rod in a time t. The current is I equals Q over t. In that same time, the charges drift the length of rod that's inside the field, so their speed is l over t. Each moving charge gets a push of q v B, so all of them together get Q times v times B. Now swap in Q equals I t, and v equals l over t. The t's cancel, and the force is B I l. Here l is only the length of wire inside the field, called the active length. Let's try it. A rod has thirty centimetres inside a field of zero point zero five zero tesla, and carries four point zero amps straight across the field. F equals B I l, so that's zero point zero five zero times four point zero times zero point three zero, which is zero point zero six zero newtons. That's about the weight of a six gram mass. Small, but enough to swing a light rod.",
